|> Exercise
    handle=_cosx_plus_sinx_as_sinusoid_

    A geometric interpretation of
    $\cos x + \sin x$ is given in Exercise >>_max_cosx_plus_sinx_new_new_ver_
    that, to recap, involves “raining down at $-45^\circ$”
    onto the $x$ axis, as illustrated here:

    |> Image
        src=images/e5_cosx_plus_sinx_as_geometric_cos_1_v2.svg

    Using this geometric insight, how can we express
    $x \ra \cos x + \sin x$ as a function of the form

    $$
    x \ra A\cdot\cos(Bx + C) + D
    $$

    for some constants $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$? (Deduce the answer
    using geometry, and, if you have the heart, verify using
    algebra.)

    |> Solution

        Imagine a large ruler living below the $x$ axis,
        off of which we read the values of $\cos x + \sin x$:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_cosx_plus_sinx_as_geometric_cos_2.svg

        (This ruler contains just the same numbers as the $x$
        axis in the same place, but need only go from $-\sqrt{2}$
        to $\sqrt{2}$, those being the minimum and maximum values
        of $\theta \ra \cos\theta + \sin\theta$, as covered in Exercise >>_max_cosx_plus_sinx_new_new_ver_.)
        Alternately, we could read values off of a ruler placed
        at $45^\circ$ through the circle...

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_cosx_plus_sinx_as_geometric_cos_3.svg

        ...and then later multiply by $\sqrt{2}$, to recover the
        original “scale” of the horizontal ruler. However, the
        value on the diagonal ruler is

        $$
        \cos(x - \eta/2)
        $$

        as one can see by rotating the entire diagram $45^\circ$
        clockwise until that ruler aligns with the $x$ axis;
        therefore,

        |> Boxed
            $$
            \cos x + \sin x = \sqrt{2}\cdot\cos(x - \eta/2)
            $$

        ...where you can think of the left-hand side of the
        equation as the value on the original “big” horizontal
        ruler (from $-\sqrt{2}$ to $\sqrt{2}$), and the value on
        the right-hand side as the coordinate read off of the “small
        diagonal ruler” re-normalized by the factor $\sqrt{2}$.
        Concretely,

        $$
        \begin{gathered}
        A = \sqrt{2},\\
        B = \up{1.3}1,\\
        C = \up{1.3}-\eta/2,\\
        D = \up{1.3}0,
        \end{gathered}
        $$

        as those parameters appear in the problem statement.

        Now for the ALGEBRAIC VERIFICATION 
        that can accompany this geometric observation, we
        use the angle-sum formula

        $$
        \cos(A + B) = \cos A\cos B - \sin A\sin B
        $$
        for cosine, giving us...
        $$
        \begin{aligned}
        &\,\, \sqrt{2}\cdot \cos(x - \eta/2) \\
        \up{1.6} =&\,\, \sqrt{2}\cdot(\cos(x)\cos(-\eta/2) - \sin(x)\sin(-\eta/2)) \\
        \up{1.6} =&\,\, \sqrt{2}\cdot(\cos(x)\sqrt{0.5} - \sin(x)(-\sqrt{0.5})) \\
        \up{1.6} =&\,\, \sqrt{2}\cdot\sqrt{0.5}\cdot(\cos x + \sin x) \\
        \up{1.6} =&\,\, \sqrt{2 \cdot 0.5}\cdot(\cos x + \sin x) \\
        \up{1.6} =&\,\, \sqrt{1}\cdot(\cos x + \sin x) \\
        \up{1.6} =&\,\, \cos x + \sin x
        \end{aligned}
        $$
        ...absolute corroboration!!!!

        |> SolutionNote

            The fact that
            $$
            \sqrt{2}\cdot \sqrt{0.5} = 1
            $$
            can also be seen from the fact that
            $$
            \sqrt{0.5} = {1\over \sqrt{2}}
            $$
            previously discussed.

        |> SolutionNote

            If we admit

            $$
            \cos(A - B) = \cos A\cos B + \sin A\sin B
            $$

            (replace $B$ by $-B$ in the angle-sum formula
            for cosine, and use the fact that $\cos(-B) = \cos B$,
            $\sin(-B) = -\sin B$ for all $B\in \rr$) into the
            “roster of known formulas”, the algebraic proof
            becomes...

            $$
            \begin{aligned}
            &\,\, \sqrt{2}\cdot \cos(x - \eta/2) \\
            \up{1.6} =&\,\, \sqrt{2}\cdot(\cos(x)\cos(\eta/2) + \sin(x)\sin(\eta/2)) \\
            \up{1.6} =&\,\, \sqrt{2}\cdot(\cos(x)\sqrt{0.5} + \sin(x)\sqrt{0.5}) \\
            \up{1.6} =&\,\, \sqrt{2}\cdot\sqrt{0.5}\cdot(\cos x + \sin x) \\
            \up{1.6} =&\,\, \cos x + \sin x
            \end{aligned}
            $$

            ...more direct, with fewer ‘$-$’ signs.

        |> SolutionNote

            Note that the graph...

            $$
            y = \sqrt{2}\cos(x - \eta/2)
            $$

            ...as it is the graph of the composition...

            |> Image
                src=images/e5_cosx_plus_sinx_as_geometric_cos_composition.svg

            ...(cf. Exercise >>_four_compositions_graphed_) 
            can be obtained from...

            $$
            y = \cos x
            $$

            ...by...

            __1. translating horizontally by $\eta/2$ to the right__

            __2. dilating vertically by $\sqrt{2}$__

            ...and, on the other hand, if we take a look at the
            previously “annotated” graph of $x \ra \cos x + \sin x$
            (cf. Exercise >>_max_cosx_plus_sinx_new_new_ver_)...

            |> Image
                src=images/e5_cosx_plus_sinx_with_max_min_info_radian_version_without_arrows.svg

            ...it certainly _looks like_ this curve could be obtained by
            translating the graph $y = \cos x$ horizontally by
            $\eta/2$ and then scaling vertically by $\sqrt{2}$. 
            And it _looks_ so because it _is_ so!
            (We know that, now, because we have proved that $\cos x + \sin x = \sqrt{2}\cos(x - \eta/2)$
            for all $x \in \rr$.)
